    {
      "title": "Turkish Envoy Alkan Meets Police Chief as Ankara Expands Security Footprint - FTL Somalia",
      "link": "https://www.ftlsomalia.com/turkish-envoy-alkan-meets-police-chief-as-ankara-expands-security-footprint/",
      "date": "Sun, 25 Oct 2026 15:53:06 GMT",
      "image": "https://ftlsomalia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Turkish-Ambassador-Somali-Police.jpg",
      "content": "Turkish Envoy Alkan Meets Police Chief as Ankara Expands Security Footprint - Somali News today Live Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Security Nationwide Turkish Envoy Alkan Meets Police Chief as Ankara Expands Security Footprint October 25, 2026 4 min read MOGADISHU , A high-level Turkish delegation led by Ambassador Ferhat Alkan visited the headquarters of the Somali Police Force on Sunday, meeting with Police Commissioner Brigadier General Asad Osman Abdullahi to reaffirm Ankara’s commitment to training and equipping Somalia’s elite Haramcad special police unit. According to a statement from the Turkish Embassy in Mogadishu, Ambassador Alkan was accompanied by Murat Daş, a Consultant Special Operations Security Director, and Commissioner Emre Dikyol, a Police Liaison Officer from the Foreign Relations Department of the Turkish General Directorate of Security, along with members of the Turkish Police Special Operations. The embassy described Turkey’s Police Special Operations Directorate as “one of Turkey’s most elite units” and confirmed that its training activities aimed at strengthening the Haramcad Special Police Forces continue “uninterrupted.” The statement also noted that Turkey’s Ministry of Interior, General Directorate of Security, Gendarmerie General Command, and Coast Guard Command are all continuing security cooperation projects with Somalia. This is not a new relationship. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the police partnership , the Somali Police Chief met with a Turkish delegation in May 2026 to discuss expanding training programs specifically for the Haramcad unit, which has received extensive Turkish support through training, equipment, and modernization. The Haramcad commandos have graduated from the TURKSOM Military Academy in Mogadishu, where they were trained in high-risk operational tactics, rapid response, and civilian protection, as detailed in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the graduation ceremonies . The Turkish Ambassador at the time, Alper Aktaş, stated in August 2026 that Turkey had trained more than 1,000 specialized personnel within the Somali Police Force, in addition to the Gorgor special forces. A Broader Diplomatic Tour Ambassador Alkan’s presence at the police headquarters is consistent with the pattern he has established since presenting his credentials to President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud in late September. He has met with Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre to discuss energy, infrastructure, fisheries, security, health, education, space, and connectivity, as reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the talks with the Prime Minister . He has visited the spaceport under construction in Warsheikh. He has engaged with the Ministry of Information and met with Somali youth at a Mogadishu café founded by former students in Turkey. The police visit, however, carries particular weight. The Haramcad unit is not a conventional police force. It is a specialized commando unit that has been at the center of Turkey’s security assistance to Somalia, alongside the Gorgor military commandos. Turkey’s investment in these units reflects a broader strategy of building elite, Turkish-trained forces that can operate alongside or independently of the larger, less cohesive Somali National Army. Critical Note The Turkish Embassy’s statement is nothing out of the ordinary, but the presence of a “Consultant Special Operations Security Director” and a police liaison officer from Ankara’s General Directorate of Security, rather than a military attaché, underscores that this is a police-to-police relationship, distinct from Turkey’s military training mission at TURKSOM. The emphasis on “uninterrupted training activities” is also significant. In a security environment where international partners frequently suspend or condition their support based on political developments, Turkey’s commitment to the Haramcad unit has been consistent. That consistency has made Ankara an indispensable partner for the Somali Police Force, but it has also given Turkey a degree of influence over a unit that operates in Somalia’s most sensitive security spaces. For Somalia, the Haramcad model offers a template for building capable, professional forces. But the unit’s effectiveness ultimately depends on its integration into a broader security architecture that includes the police, the military, and the justice system. A well-trained commando unit operating without accountability mechanisms or a functioning criminal justice system is a tool that can protect civilians or intimidate them, depending on who is giving the orders. The Turkish Embassy’s statement thanks the Somali Police Force for its hospitality. The more important question is whether the partnership it describes is building a police force that serves the public, or one that serves the government of the day. Recommended Reading On ftlsomalia.com Somali Police Chief Discusses Security Cooperation with Turkish Delegation Police Chief Graduates Haramcad Commandos at TURKSOM Academy Turkish Ambassador Continues Diplomatic Tour: Discuss with PM Hamza Energy, Security and Space Facebook X Email WhatsApp LinkedIn Somalia’s Danab Commandos Launch Operation Banooda, Kill Seven Al-Shabaab Militants You may also like Security Somalia’s Danab Commandos Launch Operation Banooda, Kill Seven Al-Shabaab Militants October 25, 2026 6 min read Security Min. Fiqi Thanks EU Amb. Máthé for Security Support as Mogadishu Pushes for Faster International Cooperation October 24, 2026 5 min read Security Deadly Oromo-Somali Clashes in Moyale Kill Nine, Displace Thousands to Kenya October 24, 2026 5 min read Latest News Former NISA Chief Alleges Abu Dhabi Plot with Deni, Madobe and Cirro as Mogadishu Escalates UAE Accusation Lawmakers Confront Defence Ministry Over Alleged Rights Violations in the Army Turkish Envoy Alkan Meets Police Chief as Ankara Expands Security Footprint BECO, Mogadishu Power and Blue Sky Announce Rate Increase Amid 70% Fuel Spike Turkish Space Agency Delegation Visits Mogadishu and Warsheikh as Defence Cooperation Expands Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process Somali U-17 Squad Celebrated at Parliament as Calls for Sports Investment Grow Hirshabelle Victory Celebrated in Mogadishu as Political Consensus Remains Elusive Oman’s Embassy Reopens in Mogadishu as Al-Umairi Presents Credentials Abiy Ahmed Calls Sea Access an Existential Issue for Ethiopia’s 130 Million People Facility for Talo and Leadership. Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. ABOUT US | PRIVACY POLICY Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia",
      "summary": "Turkish Ambassador Ferhat Alkan recently met with Somali Police Commissioner Brigadier General Asad Osman Abdullahi to reinforce Turkey's commitment to training Somalia's elite Haramcad special police unit. This meeting, part of a high-level Turkish delegation's visit to the Somali Police Force headquarters, emphasized ongoing security cooperation between Turkey and Somalia. The Turkish Embassy highlighted that training activities for the Haramcad unit, which specializes in high-risk operations, are continuing without interruption. Turkey has previously trained over 1,000 personnel within the Somali Police Force, reflecting a broader strategy to develop elite forces capable of operating independently of the Somali National Army. Ambassador Alkan's visit aligns with his diplomatic efforts since taking office, including discussions on various sectors such as energy and health. However, the effectiveness of the Haramcad unit hinges on its integration within a comprehensive security framework, raising concerns about accountability and the potential for misuse of power.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/turkish-envoy-alkan-meets-police-chief-as-ankara-expands-sec-b9f37e37eb.html"
    },
    {
      "title": "Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process - FTL Somalia",
      "link": "https://www.ftlsomalia.com/puntlands-deni-rejects-federal-election-process/",
      "date": "Sun, 25 Oct 2026 15:18:06 GMT",
      "image": "https://ftlsomalia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/PS-HSM-Nugaal-Elder.webp",
      "content": "Puntland's Deni Rejects Federal Election Process - Somali News today Live Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Politics Puntland Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process October 25, 2026 5 min read GAROWE , Puntland President Said Abdullahi Deni has issued a stark warning about Somalia’s political trajectory, accusing President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud of refusing an agreed-upon election and obstructing dialogue, while calling on all Somali stakeholders to convene urgently to prevent further deterioration. Speaking at the opening of the 59th session of Puntland’s parliament in Garowe on Saturday, Deni said his administration would not participate in the federal government-led electoral process, deepening the standoff over Somalia’s electoral framework. He called on Somalis to engage in dialogue and reach a political understanding on the country’s direction, stressing the need for an inclusive settlement that accounts for all stakeholders’ positions. “The Puntland government calls on all different parts of Somalia to come together urgently, to save Somali statehood,” Deni said. He also urged the international community to closely monitor Somalia’s political situation and support a consultative process to address the divisions. The Puntland leader accused President Mohamud of refusing to hold an agreed election and of obstructing talks on the electoral process. His remarks come as the federal government maintains that presidential elections will be held on May 15, 2027, with parliamentary elections set for April 15 of the same year. A Fractured Political Landscape Deni’s warning is the latest escalation in a prolonged dispute that has seen Puntland, Jubaland, and the opposition Somali Future Council reject the federal government’s electoral timetable and constitutional amendments. The opposition coalition has accused President Mohamud of taking unilateral decisions that threaten national unity and the federal system. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the political crisis , former Puntland President Abdirahman Mohamed Faroole has declared that there is “no legitimate government in Mogadishu,” reflecting the depth of the rift between Garowe and the federal capital. Puntland has formally rejected the constitutional amendments, arguing that they undermine the federal system and concentrate excessive power in the hands of the federal presidency, as reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of Puntland’s rejection . The political confrontation has been compounded by practical tensions. The federal government’s enforcement of the National ID requirement for domestic travel has created disruptions for Puntland residents, as NIRA does not operate registration offices in the region. Puntland has rejected the federal ID system, with Deni signing a decree in 2025 creating the Puntland Identification Authority to issue cards independently, as detailed in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the ID dispute . Hassan Sheikh Meets Puntland Elder Over Nugaal Killings Against this backdrop of political confrontation, President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud met with Puntland traditional leader Islaan Ciise Islaan Mohamed at the Presidential Palace, with discussions reportedly focusing on the deaths of 13 people along the Garmaal and Jiifle coastal areas in the Nugaal region. According to information obtained about the meeting, Islaan Ciise urged the federal government to pursue accountability, ensure justice for the victims, and protect the rights of their bereaved families. The incident has heightened concerns over civilian safety along Puntland’s coastline, particularly among fishing communities whose livelihoods depend on access to the sea. Puntland’s administration has previously said the victims were civilian fishermen and accused Turkish forces of carrying out an attack that killed people not involved in fighting. The federal government has offered a different account, with State Minister for Defence Omar Ali Abdi stating that the government was aware of military operations along the coast and that any operation against piracy would legally require federal authorization. The circumstances surrounding the strike remain contested. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the airstrikes controversy , Turkey has stated it has no information regarding airstrikes off the Puntland coast that killed nine people, amid growing controversy over attacks that reportedly targeted a mix of fishermen and individuals suspected of being part of pirate networks. Puntland later called for an independent international investigation into the strikes, as reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the demand . Puntland President Deni has addressed the escalating security situation, expressing dissatisfaction with how the strikes were conducted and emphasizing the need for legal accountability. “We are not satisfied with how things happened, and we are deeply concerned. If someone commits a crime, we do not need to take extrajudicial action; crime has a legal process to follow,” Deni stated. Critical Note Deni’s call for urgent dialogue and his decision to boycott the federal electoral process are not isolated gestures. They represent a calculated strategy by Puntland to position itself as the principal alternative center of political authority in Somalia, at a moment when the federal government’s legitimacy is contested by multiple stakeholders. The meeting between President Mohamud and Islaan Ciise is a recognition that the Nugaal killings have created a humanitarian and political crisis that cannot be ignored. But a single meeting with a traditional elder does not resolve the underlying questions about accountability, civilian protection, and the role of foreign military operations in Puntland’s territory. The families of the victims have been promised justice. Whether they receive it will depend on whether the federal government is willing to investigate an operation that Puntland believes was conducted by its Turkish partners. The two developments, Deni’s political offensive and the Nugaal killings, are connected by more than timing. They both expose the fragility of the federal compact and the extent to which Puntland’s relationship with Mogadishu has deteriorated. Deni’s warning that Somali statehood is at risk is not hyperbole. It is a description of a political landscape in which the federal government and its most powerful regional ally are moving in opposite directions, with no agreed framework for resolving their differences. The call for urgent talks is necessary. But talks require willing partners, and the gap between Garowe and Mogadishu has never been wider. Recommended Reading On ftlsomalia.com Former Puntland President Faroole: No Legitimate Government in Mogadishu Puntland Condemns Airstrikes as Turkey Denies Involvement Puntland Calls for Independent International Investigation into Deadly Airstrikes Puntland Rejects Constitutional Amendments, Says Process Lacked Consensus Puntland Rejects Federal National ID Mandate for Air Travel Facebook X Email WhatsApp LinkedIn Lawmakers Confront Defence Ministry Over Alleged Rights Violations in the Army Somali Ministers Call for Dialogue as Somaliland Elders Attend Mogadishu Event You may also like Politics Former NISA Chief Alleges Abu Dhabi Plot with Deni, Madobe and Cirro as Mogadishu Escalates UAE Accusation October 25, 2026 8 min read Politics Lawmakers Confront Defence Ministry Over Alleged Rights Violations in the Army October 25, 2026 4 min read Politics Somali Ministers Call for Dialogue as Somaliland Elders Attend Mogadishu Event October 23, 2026 4 min read Latest News Former NISA Chief Alleges Abu Dhabi Plot with Deni, Madobe and Cirro as Mogadishu Escalates UAE Accusation Lawmakers Confront Defence Ministry Over Alleged Rights Violations in the Army Turkish Envoy Alkan Meets Police Chief as Ankara Expands Security Footprint BECO, Mogadishu Power and Blue Sky Announce Rate Increase Amid 70% Fuel Spike Turkish Space Agency Delegation Visits Mogadishu and Warsheikh as Defence Cooperation Expands Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process Somali U-17 Squad Celebrated at Parliament as Calls for Sports Investment Grow Hirshabelle Victory Celebrated in Mogadishu as Political Consensus Remains Elusive Oman’s Embassy Reopens in Mogadishu as Al-Umairi Presents Credentials Abiy Ahmed Calls Sea Access an Existential Issue for Ethiopia’s 130 Million People Facility for Talo and Leadership. Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. ABOUT US | PRIVACY POLICY Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia",
      "summary": "Puntland President Said Abdullahi Deni has publicly rejected the federal election process proposed by President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, accusing him of obstructing dialogue and failing to honor agreed-upon electoral timelines. During the opening of Puntland's parliament session, Deni emphasized the need for urgent discussions among Somali stakeholders to prevent further political deterioration, asserting that his administration would not participate in the federal electoral process. This rejection reflects a broader discontent among Puntland and other regions, such as Jubaland, regarding the federal government's electoral timetable and constitutional amendments, which they claim centralize power excessively. The political tensions are compounded by recent violent incidents in Puntland, including the deaths of civilians, which have raised concerns about accountability and foreign military operations in the region. Deni's stance signals a significant rift between Puntland and the federal government, highlighting the fragility of Somalia's political landscape and the urgent need for a collaborative resolution.",
