      "content": "Fort Gordon hosts fitness event to honor fallen hero Skip to content Submit Photos/Videos Community Calendar About Us Advertising Watch Live News First Alert Weather 12 On Your Side Investigates Latest Newscasts Sports Morning Mix We the People Home Election Results Candidates Presidential Election Map Georgia Interactive Results South Carolina Interactive Results Alfonzo Williams Javeayah Harris News Local Business Crime Education National Politics Regional 12 On Your Side Investigates First Alert Weather Fish and Game Forecast 24/7 Weather First Alert Weather Radar Closings 3 Degree Guarantee Weather Specials Weather Blog Pollen Sense Weather Maps Sports Bulldawg Banter Masters Under the Lights Puppy Picks Morning Mix Aerial Augusta Finding Solutions Miracle Monday Open for Business We the People Live Camera Network Meet the Team About Us Jobs Grant Me Hope Community Calendar Back To School Contests Mug-Club Ticket Giveaway General Contest Rules Community Daily Pledge Obituaries Circle Country Local News Live InvestigateTV Watching Your Wallet PowerNation TV Listings NextGen TV Call For Action Submit Your Photos and Videos Marketplace/Advertising Creative Marketing Digital Marketing Gray AI Policy Fort Gordon hosts fitness event to honor fallen hero Master Sgt. Gary Gordon died while serving in Somalia and is the namesake of Fort Gordon. By Staff Updated : 23 hours ago Share Add Us On Google Add as a preferred source on Google FORT GORDON, Ga. (WRDW/WAGT) - Soldiers and civilians gathered at Fort Gordon on Saturday to work out while honoring a fallen hero. Master Sgt. Gary Gordon died while serving in Somalia. He is the namesake of Fort Gordon. The installation honored him with a 5K and fitness challenge. One participant said the event helps civilians learn about the culture of excellence the military strives for. “Being able to put together exercises like this, and events like this. Just to help us honor that. The military is based off of traditions and honor, so being able to share that with the community is one of our biggest things,” said First Sgt. Alexander Buza, NCOA Headquarters Branch Chief at Fort Gordon. Workouts at the event included deadlifting, sled drags and 20-foot rope climbs. Copyright 2026 WRDW/WAGT. All rights reserved. Most Read Waynesboro man dies after vehicle crash Skateboarder dead after collision with semi-truck in Aiken Woman injured, suspect arrested in shooting on Peach Orchard Road Juvenile struck by ricochet bullet during shooting on Murphy Street GBI investigating shots fired at Georgia game warden Aiken County sheriff IDs deputy who was shot, 3 suspects who were arrested 12 hurt, 11 shot following shooting after FAMU homecoming Former Ga. college basketball player injured in Laney shooting Latest News Skateboarder dead after collision with semi-truck in Aiken Audi to build $4B first US plant in Georgia Trump says tech companies should pay Georgians thousands to host data centers 12 hurt, 11 shot following shooting after FAMU homecoming Waynesboro man dies after vehicle crash “The fans deserved this;” Gamecock fans rush the field after thriller over Tennessee History comes to life at Walk with the Spirits event Authorities look for missing people across the CSRA News Live Newscasts 12 On Your Side Investigates First Alert Weather Sports About Us Contests Community TV Listings WRDW-TV/WAGT-TV 1226 Gray Way Augusta, GA 30909 (803) 278-1212 WRDW Public Inspection File WAGT Public Inspection Files FCC Liason - (762) 685-5368 FCC Applications Terms of Service Privacy Policy EEO Statement Advertising Digital Marketing Closed Captioning/Audio Description Click here to learn more about our approach to artificial intelligence. A Gray Local Media Station © 2002-2026",
      "summary": "Fort Gordon hosted a fitness event on Saturday to honor Master Sgt. Gary Gordon, who lost his life while serving in Somalia and is the installation's namesake. The event featured a 5K run and various fitness challenges, including deadlifting, sled drags, and rope climbs. Participants, comprising both soldiers and civilians, engaged in these activities to commemorate Gordon's legacy and promote the military's culture of excellence. First Sgt. Alexander Buza, the NCOA Headquarters Branch Chief at Fort Gordon, emphasized the importance of such events in sharing military traditions and values with the community. The gathering not only served as a tribute to a fallen hero but also fostered a sense of unity and understanding between the military and local residents. Overall, the event highlighted the commitment to honor those who have served and the values they represent.",
